  "worked_solution": "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=1\n$$\n\n is the equation of a ellipse with semiaxes $1$ and $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nNotice that the other equations do not define an ellipse:\n\n- ${C}_{1}\\,:\\,2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines the single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=1$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $3.$\n\n- ${C}_{3}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}^{2}=4{x}_{1}$ is the equation of a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{1}$-axis.\n\n- ${C}_{5}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ is the equation two intersecting lines in the  in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.",
